+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to your morning cup</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-intro">Good coffee starts long before we switch on the roaster. Here's how we choose our beans.</p>
+
+      <article id="journal-copy" class="journal-copy" aria-label="Our bean sourcing process">
+        <p>Our sourcing process begins with a question: who grew this coffee, and what makes their harvest worth sharing? Before a bean reaches The Daily Grind, we look beyond tasting notes to understand the farm, the growing conditions, and the people behind it. We seek out growers, cooperatives, and importing partners who can tell us where a lot comes from and how it was produced. A beautiful cup matters, but so does having a clear story behind it.</p>
+
+        <p>We start each season by tasting small samples from different origins. Altitude, soil, rainfall, and coffee variety all shape a bean's character, so we approach each lot with fresh expectations. At the cupping table, we compare aroma, sweetness, acidity, body, and finish. We taste coffees side by side and return to them as they cool, looking for balance and clarity rather than a single dramatic flavor. Only the lots we would happily brew for ourselves move to the next stage.</p>
+
+        <p>Alongside those tastings, we ask our sourcing partners about harvest practices, processing methods, and how producers are paid. We favor relationships built around traceability and open conversations about pricing, rather than relying on a label alone. Where possible, we return to the same producers across harvests. That continuity helps us understand changes in quality, gives us a chance to share useful feedback, and supports planning beyond a single purchase.</p>
+
+        <p>Processing is another important part of the story. A washed coffee may offer a crisp, clear expression of its origin, while a naturally processed lot can bring fuller fruit notes and sweetness. Neither method is automatically better; careful picking, fermentation, and drying matter more than a fashionable name. We ask how water is managed, how coffee is dried and stored, and what steps protect the surrounding land. We look for thoughtful practices and honest answers, recognizing that each region has different resources and challenges.</p>
+
+        <p>Once we select a lot, we work with our partners to preserve its quality through milling, shipping, and storage. On arrival, we sample it again to check that the coffee matches what first caught our attention. Then we develop a roast profile in small batches, adjusting time and heat to bring out the bean's natural sweetness without covering its character. We cup those trial roasts before choosing a profile, and keep tasting production batches so the coffee in your bag reflects the care that went into selecting it.</p>
+
+        <p>Our menu changes with the harvest because coffee is an agricultural product, not a fixed recipe. A favorite lot may give way to a new origin when its season ends, and we see that as an invitation to explore. Whether you reach for a familiar espresso or try a bright seasonal filter coffee, our aim stays the same: a delicious cup with a traceable journey, considered sourcing decisions, and respect for the work that happens before roasting begins.</p>
+      </article>
